ZTEXT00100001da87d1e1a8d0000000
Zero IA32 UEFI loader

loader32.zero is original Zero source compiled to PE32 by compiler.zero's
IA32 UEFI target: build/zero --efi32 < loader32.zero > build/BOOTIA32.EFI.
It opens KERNEL32.BIN from the filesystem that supplied
EFI/BOOT/BOOTIA32.EFI, allocates executable kernel pages and a separate stack,
copies display metadata, exits boot services and enters our 32-bit kernel.
No foreign loader, library or implementation is linked into this image.

IA32 ABI facts
Reference interfaces: UEFI Specification 2.10, sections 2.3.2, 4, 7, 9 and 13:
https://uefi.org/specs/UEFI/2.10/02_Overview.html#ia-32-platforms
https://uefi.org/specs/UEFI/2.10/04_EFI_System_Table.html
https://uefi.org/specs/UEFI/2.10/07_Services_Boot_Services.html
https://uefi.org/specs/UEFI/2.10/09_Protocols_EFI_Loaded_Image.html
https://uefi.org/specs/UEFI/2.10/13_Protocols_Media_Access.html

EFI_HANDLE, pointers, UINTN and EFI_STATUS are 32 bits. EFI_PHYSICAL_ADDRESS,
EFI_FILE_INFO sizes, File.Open modes/attributes and our own handoff fields
remain 64 bits. The compiler keeps full unsigned u64 expression semantics;
the IA32 firmware-call primitives marshal explicit 32-bit stack words.

Firmware uses cdecl with caller stack aligned to 16 bytes. EAX/ECX/EDX are
volatile; other general registers are preserved. Image entry receives its
handle and system-table pointer at entry ESP+4 and ESP+8. Kernel entry uses
the same stack convention for arg0, its handoff pointer. The compiler's enter
primitive installs the stack and disables interrupts before the transfer.

File.Open has seven stack words: This, NewHandle**, UTF16Path*, OpenMode low,
OpenMode high, Attributes low, Attributes high. It therefore uses call7 with
values This, output, path, 1, 0, 0, 0. SetWatchdogTimer uses five words because
its WatchdogCode is u64: timeout, code low, code high, data size, data pointer.
Every call requires exact EFI_SUCCESS (zero); positive warnings never stand
for completion. IA32 EFI_INVALID_PARAMETER is 80000002, not the x64 value.

Offsets, in bytes
SystemTable BootServices60.
BootServices AllocatePages32, GetMemoryMap40, FreePool48, HandleProtocol88,
ExitBootServices128, SetWatchdogTimer140, LocateProtocol172.
LoadedImage DeviceHandle12. SimpleFileSystem OpenVolume8.
FileProtocol Open8, Close12, Read20, GetInfo36.
GOP QueryMode0, SetMode4, Mode12.
GOP mode MaxMode0, Mode4, Info8, SizeOfInfo12, FrameBufferBase16 (u64),
FrameBufferSize24 (UINTN/u32).
EFI_FILE_INFO FileSize8 is u64 on both architectures.

Native compiler bundle
After loading the kernel, the loader opens TOOLS.BIN from the same root.
GetInfo must report exactly1MiB; bounded partial reads fill256 LoaderCode
pages. The ZRBNDL01/version1/size1048576/count6 header is checked before exit.
It then allocates2048 LoaderCode pages (8MiB) for native compiler buffers,
stack and generated executable code. The bundle may start no higher than
ffeff000 and workspace no higher thanff7ff000; both obey the sameffffefff
allocation ceiling. Source/runtime/app layout is in portability/guest/BUNDLE.ztext.

Allocation and handoff
AllocatePages uses AllocateMaxAddress with maximum address ffffefff so the
code, stack, map and all their end addresses are representable by 32-bit
pointers. Kernel code has120 pages (480KiB), stack pool256 pages (1MiB), and
handoff/map 17 pages (68 KiB). Failed allocations retain their firmware
status; output-address bounds are validated only after success, and failed
allocation outputs are never used as pointers.
The120-page code allocation must start no higher thanfff87000; its end is
at mostfffff000, leaving the same top4KiB guard as the other allocations.

The handoff is cleared for 256 bytes. All fields remain u64 for compatibility
with the shared kernel source and BIOS loader: memory-map size0, stride8,
map pointer16, code pages24, map key32, descriptor version40; display kind48,
framebuffer56, width64, height72, stride pixels80, pixel format88, boot kind96
(2 for UEFI), architecture104 (32 for this loader), stack-pool base112 and
pool size120 (1048576), bundle base128/size136 (1048576), workspace
base144/size152 (8388608). The main stack starts at the top of this allocation;
the kernel carves two child stacks from its lower end. Firmware writes only
the low 32 bits of UINTN outputs; cleared high halves remain zero.

Display and errors
GOP mode choice prefers1920x1080, then800x600/640x480 or another compatible
mode, with the bounded selection and fallback policy described
in UEFI-CONSOLE.ztext. Validation and QueryMode buffer release happen before
the final GetMemoryMap/ExitBootServices loop. RGB/BGR 32-bit pixel layouts are
supported. The framebuffer must start below 4 GiB and its full claimed range
must end no higher than 4 GiB; the kernel never truncates a high framebuffer
address into an unrelated low address. Unsupported display leaves kind0
for serial use. COM1 is optional; absence or timeout disables diagnostics.

After the first exit attempt only GetMemoryMap and ExitBootServices may call
firmware. The preallocated map is bounded to 64 KiB. Only invalid-key errors
are retried, at most four attempts. Failure diagnostics use direct UART I/O.
Read completion is a separate flag, short reads are accumulated, zero-progress
reads fail, and no code beyond the validated480KiB size is read. Successful
GetInfo must report80..512 bytes before FileSize is examined.

Historical 0.4.0 integration evidence
An independent source/ABI review checked the offsets, argument widths,
address bounds and exit discipline. The migrated source compiled to a
20480-byte PE32 IA32 EFI application using the earlier bootstrap compiler.
The current build command is the unified --efi32 command above; self-hosted
compiler and current release checks are recorded in verification.ztext.

A real IA32 OVMF boot under QEMU pc/TCG, -cpu qemu32, 128 MiB RAM loaded that
application from our own multi-file FAT16 image. It opened KERNEL32.BIN,
finished ExitBootServices, entered the shared kernel compiled for 32-bit x86,
mounted ZeroFS and reached the visible GOP terminal. PS/2 key injection typed
save ia32 works and read ia32; the kernel reported a disk save and read works.
COM1 mirrored these messages. A captured screen independently shows them.
This is a firmware/loader integration test; final combined-ISO regression
results are recorded in verification.ztext. Physical hardware and native
VirtualBox execution are separate, unverified environments.

Test inputs/results are in /workspace/uefi32-test/boot for this workspace:
normal.serial, normal.png and the private boot/data/firmware-variable copies.
The test VM was stopped. Existing packaged data.img was not modified.
IA32 OVMF (Debian ovmf-ia32 2025.02-8+deb13u1) is an external firmware test
dependency and is not part of our OS or distributed as our implementation.

Historical384KiB growth validation is recorded in
verification/boot-growth-0.7.0/results.ztext. An IA32 OVMF boot loaded markers
through the final byte393215, verified code-pages96, wrote the full64KiB arena
and its allocated stack pool, then signaled success after ExitBootServices.

The historical 448 KiB capacity-only probes remain in
verification/boot-capacity-448k/. Current 480 KiB slots and the native compiler
bundle are checked in verification/guest-bundle-480k/; the probe verifies the
whole 1 MiB loaded bundle, every 8 MiB workspace page and the final kernel
byte on BIOS/UEFI, both 32- and 64-bit. Native compiler execution evidence is
separate in verification/guest-app/bundle/. Final OS integration results
remain separate in verification.ztext.
